April 2003
For the set of 17 diverse, neutral organic molecules, we have reexamined 
optimal scaling factors for CM charges by fitting to computed absolute free
energies of hydration from FEP calculations:
charge model    scaling factor   AUE            Worst case
   CM1A             1.14        1.11 kcal/mol     nitro
   CM3A             1.15        1.28              amides

CM1P and CM3P yield poorer results with significant problems for N-containing molecules.

January 2003
The ability to calculate CM3 charges for AM1 and PM3 has been implemented. Reference:
J. D. Thompson, C. J. Cramer, and D. G. Truhlar, J. Comput. Chem. 24, 1291-1304 (2003).
We have not yet determined the optimal scaling factor for CM3 charges;
for now, we are assuming that it will be 1.08 as for CM1A charges for neutral
molecules. You can obtain unscaled CM3 charges with the scripts
xAM1CM3 and xPM3CM3. The xPDG??? scripts generate CM3 charges.
Chris Cramer kindly confirmed the correctness of our CM3 results in comparison
with AMSOL results. He also expressed preference for the AM1/CM3 charges
feeling that nitrogen is fundamentally too electropositive with PM3.

January 22, 2001
I completed the FEP calculations for 17 molecules in TIP4P water.
This is the original set (JPC-B 1998, 102, 1787) plus propane, dimethylamine, 
NMA, and nitroethane. The mean unsigned errors for free energies of hydration
for 16 molecules (excluding nitroethane) are:

  charge set     error(kcal/mol)
  1.20*CM1A       1.53
  1.10*CM1A       0.91
  1.08*CM1A       0.90
  1.00*CM1A       1.33
  1.30*CM1P       1.62 (from Marina - N=13; original set only)

including nitroethane the errors are

  charge set     error(kcal/mol)
  1.20*CM1A       1.82
  1.10*CM1A       1.12
  1.08*CM1A       1.08
  1.00*CM1A       1.39
  1.30*CM1P       1.94 (from Marina - N=14)                    

On this basis, we will use 1.08*CM1A as our default charge set
from now on.
The xAM1OPT and xAM1SP scripts now use the 1.08 scale factor
for neutral molecules; still use 1.0 for charged molecules.

